<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: The Silk Road</h1><p class="ws-lede">Follow silk, ideas and armies along the routes that bound Eurasia together.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/silk-road">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Mongol Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Tang Dynasty</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Parthian Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Han Dynasty</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Timurid Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Battle of Talas</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Caravan trade</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Papermaking</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Silk Road</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Han Dynasty</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Zhang Qian's missions and Emperor Wu's western campaigns opened the routes, and Han garrisons in the Tarim Basin kept them running.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 5, <b>Tang Dynasty</b>: &#8220;Tang Chang'an, with perhaps a million people, hosted Sogdian, Persian, Arab and Indian merchants in its two great markets.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 9, <b>Timurid Empire</b>: &#8220;Caravan trade between China, India and the Mediterranean enriched Samarkand, Bukhara and Herat, a late flowering of the overland routes.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Follow silk, ideas and armies along the routes that bound Eurasia together.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/silk-road. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: The Silk Road</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Parthian Empire <span>from 247 BCE</span></li><li>Han Dynasty <span>from 206 BCE</span></li><li>Tang Dynasty <span>from 618 CE</span></li><li>Battle of Talas <span>751</span></li><li>Mongol Empire <span>from 1206 CE</span></li><li>Timurid Empire <span>from 1363 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Caravan trade</dt><dd>Caravan trade is long-distance overland commerce carried by groups of merchants with pack animals, typically camels, travelling together for safety.</dd><dt>Papermaking</dt><dd>Papermaking is the craft of producing paper from plant fibres, invented in China.</dd><dt>Silk Road</dt><dd>The Silk Road was the network of overland trade routes linking China with Central Asia, Iran, the Middle East and the Mediterranean.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
